  "contentPlaintext": "<bold>Project Proposal</bold>\nThis document serves as the formal initiation artifact for the Senior Project. It defines the core problem statement, project scope, proposed technical methodology, timeline milestones, and risk mitigation strategies for faculty review.\n<bold>1. Table of Contents</bold>\n1. Table of Contents\n2. Executive Summary & Problem Identification\n3. Aims, Objectives & Project Scope\n4. Proposed System Architecture & Methodology\n5. High-Level Project Plan & Milestone Breakdown\n6. Feasibility Study & Risk Management\n7. Resource Requirements & Faculty Commitments\n<bold>2. Executive Summary & Problem Identification</bold>\n<bold>Project Title</bold>\n<italic>[Provide a concise, technically descriptive title for the project]</italic>\n<bold>Abstract / Executive Summary</bold>\n<italic>[Provide a comprehensive 200–250 word summary of the project. Clearly state the problem, the proposed engineering solution, and the anticipated real-world impact or contribution of the system.]</italic>\n<bold>Problem Statement & Context</bold>\n<bold>Current State Analysis:</bold> Describe the existing ecosystem, workflows, or baseline technologies currently addressing this domain. Highlight where they fail.\n<bold>Identified Critical Gaps:</bold> Detail the specific inefficiencies, computational bottlenecks, high operational costs, or missing functionalities that justify a new system.\n<bold>Target Audience / User Persona:</bold> Explicitly name who experiences this problem and who will directly benefit from the engineered solution.\n<bold>3. Aims, Objectives & Project Scope</bold>\n<bold>Primary Project Aim</bold>\n<italic>[State the overarching macro-goal of your system in one clear, ambitious, yet technically grounded sentence.]</italic>\n<bold>Measurable Technical Objectives</bold>\nTo achieve the primary aim, the team will complete the following specific, measurable engineering targets:\n<bold>      Objective 1 (Research & Design):</bold> e.g., Design a low-latency, scalable database schema capable of processing 1,000 concurrent transactions.\n<bold>      Objective 2 (Implementation):</bold> e.g., Implement an automated, cross-platform mobile user interface using React Native with sub-200ms view updates.\n<bold>      Objective 3 (Data/Logic):</bold> e.g., Train an optimized machine learning model achieving a minimum of 85% classification accuracy on the selected dataset.\n<bold>      Objective 4 (Testing/Evaluation):</bold> e.g., Execute automated end-to-end integration tests yielding a test coverage score of at least 80%.\n<bold>Scope Boundaries</bold>\n<bold>In-Scope Features & System Capabilities</bold>\n<bold>     Authentication & Security:</bold> e.g., Secure multi-factor authentication and role-based access control (RBAC).\n<bold>     Core Functional Module A:</bold> Detailed description of the primary operational engine of the software.\n<bold>     Core Functional Module B:</bold> Detailed description of data pipelines, rendering engines, or processing systems.\n<bold>      Data Analysis & Export:</bold> Detailed description of reporting engines, PDF/CSV extraction, or telemetry dashboards.\n<bold>Out-of-Scope (Explicit System Exclusions)</bold>\n<bold>     Production Hosting Scaling:</bold> The system will remain configured for local simulation or limited staging environments rather than worldwide server deployment.\n<bold>     Third-Party Payment Gates:</bold> Financial elements will be emulated via sandbox environments; live financial banking rails will not be integrated.\n<bold>     Unsupported Platforms:</bold> The application will be optimized specifically for desktop web browsers and will exclude native desktop operating system installations.\n<bold>4. Proposed System Architecture & Methodology</bold>\n<bold>Preliminary Conceptual Block Diagram</bold>\n┌─────────────────┐       ┌────────────────────────┐       ┌─────────────────────┐\n│  Client Tier    │ ───\\  │      Api / Logic       │ ───\\  │    Database Tier    │\n│ (Web Frontend)  │ ───/  │ (Node.js/Python Micro) │ ───/  │ (Relational Data)   │\n└─────────────────┘       └────────────────────────┘       └─────────────────────┘\n<bold>Anticipated Software & Hardware Stack</bold>\n<bold>     Frontend Technologies:</bold> e.g., TypeScript, Next.js, TailwindCSS.\n<bold>     Backend & API Layer:</bold> e.g., Python FastAPI, GraphQL, Docker Engine.\n<bold>     Persistence & Caching Tiers:</bold> e.g., PostgreSQL (relational), Redis (session caching).\n<bold>     Development Tools:</bold> e.g., Git, GitHub Actions, Postman, Jest Testing Framework.\n<bold>Engineering Methodology</bold>\nThe project will be executed using a <bold>Modified Agile (Scrum) Methodology</bold>, customized to fit the rigid milestone framework of the academic calendar:\n<bold>     Sprint Cycles:</bold> Fixed 2-week internal development sprints culminating in a team code review.\n<bold>     Verification Gates:</bold> Every milestone document undergoes a mandatory internal peer evaluation before formal supervisor submission.\n<bold>5. High-Level Project Plan & Milestone Breakdown</bold>\nWk 1-4: Requirements ➔ Wk 5-8: System Design ➔ Wk 9-12: Implementation v1 ➔ Wk 13-16: Evaluation & Defense\nPhase ID\nFaculty Milestone Artifact\nPrimary Scope / Focus Area\nExpected Delivery Date\n<bold>M1</bold>\nProject Proposal\nInitial scope verification, risk analysis, and team baseline.\nWeek 4\n<bold>M2</bold>\nRequirements Analysis (RAD)\nFormal software requirements specification (SRS) and use cases.\nWeek 7\n<bold>M3</bold>\nSystem Design (RDD)\nComprehensive architectural maps, data models, and API definitions.\nWeek 10\n<bold>M4</bold>\nTest Plan & Evaluation\nSystem validation, regression analysis, and performance stress testing.\nWeek 13\n<bold>M5</bold>\nFinal Project Report & Code\nCapstone defense, code bundle handover, and final grading.\nWeek 16\n<bold>6. Feasibility Study & Risk Management</bold>\n<bold>Feasibility Assessment</bold>\n<bold>     Technical Feasibility:</bold> The team possesses core competencies in Python and web development. Missing infrastructure knowledge (DevOps pipelines) will be mitigated through targeted research in Weeks 1–3.\n<bold>     Operational Feasibility:</bold> The project does not rely on active business partnerships; all simulated data profiles can be synthetically generated inside the testing suite.\n<bold>     Economic / Cost Feasibility:</bold> All selected frameworks, developer databases, and cloud development environments operate on free open-source tiers. Total financial cost to the team is $0.00.\n<bold>Risk Log & Mitigation Strategies</bold>\nRisk Identifier\nRisk Description\nImpact\nProbability\nProactive Mitigation Strategy\n<bold>R-01</bold>\nTeam member falls ill or leaves the university.\nHigh\nLow\nCode is pushed daily to main repository. Documentation is central so task ownership can shift instantly.\n<bold>R-02</bold>\nChosen third-party API is deprecated or experiences heavy rate limits.\nMedium\nMedium\nWrap the external integration in an abstraction layer to allow quick switching to an alternative provider.\n<bold>R-03</bold>\nCore algorithm latency exceeds maximum acceptable threshold (e.g., >2s).\nHigh\nMedium\nProfile processing loops weekly. Allocate internal sprint buffer for caching mechanics if needed.\n<bold>7. Resource Requirements & Faculty Commitments</bold>\n<bold>Hardware Resources Required</bold>\nStandard laptop workstations for local software development and model training.\nOn-campus laboratory infrastructure for physical integration testing (if applicable).\n<bold>Software & Cloud Infrastructure Needed</bold>\nAccess to university cloud instances (AWS/Azure) or standard local containers (Docker).\nAcademic API licenses or dataset access authorizations from faculty archives.\n<bold>Faculty / Supervisor Assistance Expected</bold>\nWeekly 30-minute review meetings to assess compliance with faculty rubrics.\nIntroduction or referral to internal department subject matter experts for complex domain issues.",
